# Code Style

## Indentation

Use **2-space** indentation everywhere. Never use tabs or 4-space indent.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
function save() {
    return db.write();
}

// ✅ GOOD
function save() {
  return db.write();
}
```

## Spacing

Prefer breathing room over dense blocks:

- Blank line between logical sections and between top-level declarations
- Blank line before `return` when the function body has multiple steps
- Space after commas and around operators (`a + b`, `x, y`)
- Avoid cramming multiple statements on one line

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
function loadUser(id: string) {
  const user = await db.find(id); if (!user) throw new Error("missing"); return user;
}

// ✅ GOOD
function loadUser(id: string) {
  const user = await db.find(id);

  if (!user) {
    throw new Error("missing");
  }

  return user;
}
```

## Function comments

Every function (including arrow functions assigned to names, methods, and exported helpers) gets a short comment describing **what it does**. Prefer a one-line `/** ... */` above the declaration.

```typescript
// ❌ BAD
export function formatCents(cents: number) {
  return (cents / 100).toFixed(2);
}

// ✅ GOOD
/** Format integer cents as a two-decimal dollar string. */
export function formatCents(cents: number) {
  return (cents / 100).toFixed(2);
}
```

Skip only trivial one-liner callbacks passed inline (e.g. `.map(x => x.id)`).
